#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e38689-c27a-5338-8bd1-04f4ca79a3e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163635} {:answer/id #uuid "b2207ce5-c9bc-54a0-a54a-0cca15476fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163636} {:answer/id #uuid "eb21d7db-28a1-5f86-82d4-e22597b7c44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163637} {:answer/id #uuid "05731aa0-0374-5fe3-a1c2-ab28f968251c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163638} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea376cd-3327-51c6-b153-d2ec797a56ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ea376cd-3327-51c6-b153-d2ec797a56ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163639} :answer-field/id #uuid "28623b2b-d1e6-5f74-97d9-7a05ff68f591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0706b1eb-0c66-56aa-86af-d962b1e95896" :question/math-academy-id "q-136824" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -3{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge -3{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -3{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-3(1)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-3.$ But before we conclude that $-3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-3.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-3(1)^{2}-(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6cad361-a211-5f14-a2b6-3f973404f90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163647} {:answer/id #uuid "e97eb615-e7d4-50ea-8ecd-eee2acf82b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163648} {:answer/id #uuid "0179c480-8d18-5da0-8f0d-c12d768e1948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163649} {:answer/id #uuid "0bbf4088-4ea0-57b3-a788-ee20f7ba5765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163650} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9df969-1cbb-5043-b216-cd757143e38c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0179c480-8d18-5da0-8f0d-c12d768e1948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163649} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1099cb-2025-50d3-9120-cf27fc9442e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6524f432-8e1b-53f9-ae30-319b701fe5f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-136826" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,7,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "783b1f87-c0c1-52ab-b346-f4b0e7ffa15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379820} {:answer/id #uuid "af575672-518e-5c18-97a4-7800770c87bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379821} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9bbfe3-7cda-58d8-9e59-d2b23e980625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379822} {:answer/id #uuid "570fa96e-3f08-52ec-a22c-f6cbf778bccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379823} {:answer/id #uuid "403c9b07-0dbf-5f77-9560-97c8f7accf6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "783b1f87-c0c1-52ab-b346-f4b0e7ffa15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379820} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1a0e64-0272-581c-b947-b7159d4ba4d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e597502b-9616-570d-85d2-53bc922c89a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-138021" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-4{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72fa2eec-6654-534c-a590-986e55e83815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379827} {:answer/id #uuid "436bda34-cb0d-5656-bab6-32c9314c9e95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379828} {:answer/id #uuid "afa5ab4c-d59f-5f0f-b646-b00c93517035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379829} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef97c89-1186-547d-b913-95f149ca450b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379830} {:answer/id #uuid "96a49ce8-3f28-5914-84bf-3b936394fe87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72fa2eec-6654-534c-a590-986e55e83815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379827} :answer-field/id #uuid "181f6ac5-2654-51a3-9fe9-66b5cd5682ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f07b8c52-650b-58ad-8dad-c02bf167a0ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-138075" :question/problem "Find the  maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63be96b9-041a-53cd-aecf-b20ee1f44375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379834} {:answer/id #uuid "b50f7c23-2861-5c61-9b1a-75dc826db19f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379835} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a8027a-b384-551a-9e90-c6f1eb741c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379836} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8be8fd-4e39-54e9-b47b-0d3aa3dc7cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379837} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb6a164-f073-5ef0-9012-24ee58aa1fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63be96b9-041a-53cd-aecf-b20ee1f44375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379834} :answer-field/id #uuid "b937ceeb-0702-54ca-b035-2404b89bb3d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab8bdada-0761-5c9a-8f04-e9214224aca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-158533" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n6{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{2}^{2},\\,4{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=3.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}+6(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $3.$"}]}
